.TH MAKEKEEPER 1 "October 2026" "makekeeper 0.1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
makekeeper \- A safer installer for Makefile-based Linux applications

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B makekeeper
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
makekeeper provides a controlled way to install applications that use GNU Make build systems. It tracks all installed files and managed symlinks, enabling safe uninstallation and preventing conflicts with existing installations.

Applications are installed to
.IR /opt/makekeeper/apps/<name>/
and executable symlinks are created in
.IR /usr/local/bin/ .

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B inspect [PROJECT]
Inspect a project's Makefile and preview installation commands. This shows possible Make targets and a dry-run preview of the installation without actually installing.

.TP
.B install [PROJECT] [OPTIONS]
Build and install a project. The project is built using
.BR make ,
staged to a temporary directory, checked for conflicts, and then installed.

.RS
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-name [NAME]
Specify a custom application name. Defaults to the project directory name.

.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.RE

.TP
.B git [URL] [OPTIONS]
Clone a git repository and install it. This command requires root privileges. The repository is cloned to a temporary directory, then installed using the same process as
.BR install .
The cloned directory is cleaned up after installation.

.RS
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-name [NAME]
Specify a custom application name. Defaults to the repository name.

.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.RE

.TP
.B list
List all registered applications with file and symlink counts.

.TP
.B info [NAME]
Display detailed information about an installed application, including source, prefix, installed files, and managed symlinks.

.TP
.B uninstall [NAME] [OPTIONS]
Remove tracked files and managed executable symlinks. Only unchanged files are removed; modified files are preserved for manual review.

.RS
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.RE

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help information.

.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information.

.SH EXAMPLES

Install a project:
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo makekeeper install /path/to/project
.fi
.RE

Install with a custom name:
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo makekeeper install /path/to/project --name myapp
.fi
.RE

Install from a git repository:
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo makekeeper git https://github.com/user/project
.fi
.RE

Inspect before installing:
.RS
.PP
.nf
makekeeper inspect /path/to/project
.fi
.RE

List installed applications:
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo makekeeper list
.fi
.RE

View installation details:
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo makekeeper info myapp
.fi
.RE

Uninstall an application:
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo makekeeper uninstall myapp
.fi
.RE

.SH FILES

.TP
.I /opt/makekeeper/apps/<name>/
Installation prefix for applications.

.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/
Directory where executable symlinks are created.

.TP
.I /var/lib/makekeeper/<name>.json
Installation records tracking files and symlinks.

.SH SECURITY

makekeeper requires root privileges for system-wide installation. Makefiles can execute arbitrary code; only install projects you trust. Always inspect a project with makekeeper inspect before installing. The make install step is not sandboxed.

.SH REQUIREMENTS

\- Linux system
.br
\- GNU Make
.br
\- Rust toolchain (for building makekeeper)
.br
\- Root/sudo access for installation and management

.SH SEE ALSO

.BR make (1)

.SH AUTHOR
